      title: 'Dreeg (NÃ¼rnberger Dreck)',
      subtitle: 'Trick-taking rules and play flow',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Dreeg (NÃ¼rnberger Dreck)',
            subtitle: 'Trick-taking rules and play flow',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Dreeg (a Franconian dialect form of 'Dreck', meaning trash or muck) is a complex, multiphase card game from the Nuremberg region of Germany. It is a composite game consisting of eight distinct sub-games, all based on the mechanics of Sechsundsechzig (66). Players compete to erase 'strokes' from their slate, and the game is strictly one of elimination: in each phase, players drop out as they succeed, until only one 'loser' remains to face the next challenge.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Dreeg is deeply rooted in the Franconian tavern culture of North Bavaria. It evolved as a way to extend the standard game of 66 into a longer, social competition suitable for a 'Stammtisch' (regular's table).",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Ideally 4 players. Can be played by 3.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '24-card German-suited deck (Bavarian or Franconian pattern). Card ranks: Ace (11), Ten (10), King (4), Ober (3), Unter (2), Nine (0).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deck of cards, chalk slates (Schiefertafeln) for each player, chalk.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer deals 6 cards to each player (in batches of 3). In some phases, a trump card is turned up; in others, trumps are determined by bidding.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Stroke notation: Some play with Roman III or a crossed seven instead of XII for the seven strokes; first stroke erased differently. Kartl-Akademie Weinzierlein uses 10 strokes (XX) per phase instead of 7.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Two-player trump indicator: In two-player 66 some use the 13th card as trump indicator under the talon; others use the bottom card of the pack turned up and slid under the talon.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Rot-Assen scoring (Bamberger): Bamberger variant: lone player always erases 3 strokes if successful in Rot-Assen, irrespective of opponents' card points; opponents still erase 1, 2 or 3 if they win.",
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
