        rules: {
          objective: 'Avoid being the last player with strokes left on your slate at the end of each of the eight phases.',
          gameplay: 'The game consists of 8 Phases:\n1-2. Einfacher Dreck: Standard 66 play. Players drop out as they reach 66 points in a deal.\n3. Hosen Runter: Played with open cards.\n4. Scharfer Dreck: Strict 66 with no declarations allowed.\n5. Rufer/Bettel: Players bid to win or lose a specific number of tricks.\n6-8. Special Phases: Usually includes Solo (a player plays against the rest), Wenz (only Unters are trumps), and a final elimination round.',
          keyRules: [
            "The 'Last Trick' is vital for tiebreaking but does not award points like in standard 66.",
            'Marriages: Declaring King and Ober of the same suit scores 20 (or 40 if trump).',
            'Elimination: In most phases, the last person remaining (who failed to erase their strokes) is the official loser of that phase.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "In the early phases, play defensively to erase your strokes as quickly as possible and drop out before the game becomes 'sharp'.",
          intermediate: "Counting to 66: Always track the point value of the tricks you have taken. Reaching 66 allows you to 'close' the deck and stop opponents from drawing better cards.",
          advanced: 'Slate Management: Observe how many strokes opponents have left. If a player is on their last stroke, do not feed them high-point cards (Aces/Tens).',
          tips: [
            "The Nine is a 'trash' card with 0 points, but it is excellent for safe leads when you want an opponent to lead a high card.",
            'Use your marriages (King/Ober) early to jumpstart your score toward 66.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
